136 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 114 0 R
/T (PS-022: eBPF/XDP Rate Limiting for DNS Query Floods in a Lab.)
/E (PS-022: eBPF/XDP Rate Limiting for DNS Query Floods in a Lab.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
137 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 114 0 R
/T (High-volume UDP DNS query floods saturate Linux servers because the operating system kernel spends substantial CPU resources allocating socket buffers before packets reach user space. eXpress Data Path \(XDP\) runs eBPF bytecode directly inside the network interface driver to process or drop packets before kernel memory allocation. In this project, you will build an XDP filter on a Linux host pair that performs bounded UDP DNS parsing. By applying per-source and global rate limits across three synthetic query flood patterns, you will benchmark CPU utilization, packet drops, and legitimate query success against both nftables and no-filter baselines.)
/E (High-volume UDP DNS query floods saturate Linux servers because the operating system kernel spends substantial CPU resources allocating socket buffers before packets reach user space. eXpress Data Path \(XDP\) runs eBPF bytecode directly inside the network interface driver to process or drop packets before kernel memory allocation. In this project, you will build an XDP filter on a Linux host pair that performs bounded UDP DNS parsing. By applying per-source and global rate limits across three synthetic query flood patterns, you will benchmark CPU utilization, packet drops, and legitimate query success against both nftables and no-filter baselines.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
